## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design for special structure (palindromic substrings) and reconstruction under strict non-overlap.

## Key Concepts
- Palindromic DP (isPal)
- 2D DP with exact k blocks
- Path reconstruction and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalK(s, k, c){
  const n=s.length;
  const isPal=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(false));
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      if(s[i]===s[j] && (j-i<2 || isPal[i+1][j-1])) isPal[i][j]=true;
    }
  }
  // DP placeholders
  return null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize to O(n^2) space or improve with monotone queues?
- How would you adapt if exact k is relaxed to at most k blocks?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem evaluates proficiency in tree DP with disjoint-path packing and exact-path-count constraints, along with path reconstruction. It combines knapsack-on-tree techniques with path endpoint handling while guaranteeing vertex-disjointness.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP with multiple states per node (unused vs. endpoint vs. interior)
- Knapsack-style merging across children via DP table convolution
- Path reconstruction through stored decision pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfs(u, parent) {
    const dp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
    dp0[u][0] = dp1[u][1] = 0;
    for (const v of children[u]) {
        dfs(v, u);
        const ndp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
        for (let i = 0; i <= k; i++) for (let j = 0; j + i <= k; j++) {
            ndp0[i + j] = Math.max(ndp0[i + j], dp0[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp1[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp1[i + j] = Math.max(ndp1[i + j], dp1[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp2[i + j] = Math.max(ndp2[i + j], dp2[u][i] + dp0[v][j]);
        }
        dp0 = ndp0; dp1 = ndp1; dp2 = ndp2;
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests dynamic programming with multiple constraints: non-adjacent blocks, exact block count, and reconstruction requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Disjoint blocks with at least one-element gap between consecutive blocks
- dp[i][t] transitions for skipping elements vs. ending a block
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sum queries
- Backpointers for reconstructing the chosen subarray endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const prefix = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) prefix[i] = prefix[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip element i
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        prev[i][t] = { skipped: true };
      }
      // End a block at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const prevIdx = s >= 2 ? s - 2 : 0;
        const val = dp[prevIdx][t - 1] + (prefix[i] - prefix[s - 1]) - c;
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = { skipped: false, start: s };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    const state = prev[i][t];
    if (state.skipped) {
      i--;
    } else {
      result.push({ start: state.start, end: i });
      i = state.start - 2;
      t--;
    }
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: result.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests budgeted DP on strings and reconstruction. 

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][j][d] encodes a subproblem under a deletion budget; 
- palindrome subsequence logic with matches/skips; 
- reconstruction paths under deletion budget.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalWithDeletions(s, k){
  const n=s.length; 
  const dp=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ dp[i][i][0]=1; for(let d=1; d<=k; d++) dp[i][i][d]=1; }
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++){
      const j=i+len-1;
      for(let d=0; d<=k; d++){
        if(s[i]===s[j]){
          dp[i][j][d]=Math.max(dp[i+1][j-1][d]+2, (d>0?dp[i+1][j][d-1]:0), (d>0?dp[i][j-1][d-1]:0));
        } else {
          dp[i][j][d]=Math.max(dp[i+1][j][d], dp[i][j-1][d], (d>0?dp[i+1][j-1][d-1]:0));
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...Array.from({length:k+1},(_,d)=>dp[0][n-1][d]));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can space be reduced to O(n^2)?
- How to reconstruct the deletion indices?
- Change objective to count distinct palindromic subsequences with budget.

## Why This Is Asked
Offers a new DP angle: path with exact turns, combining grid path DP with turn accounting. Tests deriving a tight state space, base cases, and reconstruction, mirroring routing-like constraints in real systems.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with an extra dimension for turns
- Two direction states to capture turns
- Reconstruction via backpointers
- Careful base/edge-case handling (start cell, negative infinities)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  // 4D DP: [i][j][t][d]
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () =>
    Array.from({length: K+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])));
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        // last move right
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(
            (dp[i][j-1][t][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t][0] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
        // last move down
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(
            (dp[i-1][j][t][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t][1] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n-1][m-1][K][0], dp[n-1][m-1][K][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to allow at most k turns and also maximize path length if grid values included negative numbers?
- How would you optimize space to O(nmK) memory or better, and what would be the trade-offs for reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic programming with a non-adjacency constraint and explicit reconstruction of the deletion indices.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two states per index to enforce non-adjacency
- Exact deletions constraint (k) and reconstruction
- O(nk) time and O(nk) space (can optimize to O(k) with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumAfterDeletions(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const keep = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const del = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  keep[0][0] = A[0];
  if (k >= 1) del[0][1] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      keep[i][t] = Math.max(keep[i-1][t], del[i-1][t]);
      if (keep[i][t] > NEG/2) keep[i][t] += A[i];
      if (t > 0) del[i][t] = keep[i-1][t-1];
    }
  }
  const end = Math.max(keep[n-1][k], del[n-1][k]);
  // Backtracking to collect deleted indices would be added here
  return {maxSum: end, deletedIndices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact deleted indices efficiently? 
- Can you reduce space to O(k) and still reconstruct? 
- How does the approach change if adjacent deletions are allowed at a cost?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a 3D DP on a grid with an exact count constraint and path reconstruction, a realistic routing/optimization pattern.

## Key Concepts
- DP on (i,j,t) with 3 dimensions
- Exact-count handling via -inf initialization
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Space optimizations via rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithBoosts(grid, boost, K){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const neg = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, () => Array.from({length:m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(neg)));
  const par = Array.from({length:n}, () => Array.from({length:m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(null)));
  const b00 = boost[0][0] ? 1 : 0;
  dp[0][0][b00] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for (let j=0;j<m;j++){
      for (let t=0;t<=K;t++){
        const add = boost[i][j] ? 1 : 0;
        if (i===0 && j===0) continue;
        const candFromTop = i>0 ? dp[i-1][j][t - add] : neg;
        const candFromLeft = j>0 ? dp[i][j-1][t - add] : neg;
        const bestPrev = Math.max(candFromTop, candFromLeft);
        if (bestPrev > neg/2) {
          dp[i][j][t] = bestPrev + grid[i][j];
          par[i][j][t] = candFromTop >= candFromLeft ? ['top'] : ['left'];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n-1][m-1][K], path: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover the actual path coordinates efficiently?
- How would you optimize memory to O(m*K) and still reconstruct the path?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with a 3D state on a grid path, a natural but less common extension of standard grid DP.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP on a grid, path constraints (only right/down), exact boosts
- Bottom-up iteration, reconstruction pointers, space/time considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxBoostOnGrid(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        const fromTop = i > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t] : NEG;
        const fromLeft = j > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t] : NEG;
        let best = Math.max(fromTop, fromLeft);
        if (best > NEG/2) dp[i][j][t] = best + grid[i][j];
        if (t > 0) {
          const hotTop = i > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1] : NEG;
          const hotLeft = j > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1] : NEG;
          const bestPrev = Math.max(hotTop, hotLeft);
          if (bestPrev > NEG/2) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], bestPrev + 2*grid[i][j]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n-1][m-1][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize space if k is large but grid values are small?
- How would you reconstruct the boosted cell coordinates?

## Why This Is Asked

This question introduces a non-linear block value that combines sum and minimum, forcing a DP that accounts for subarray metrics and partition points. It also tests reconstruction of exact k segments and handling of large intermediate data.

## Key Concepts

- Non-linear block cost: (sum) * (min)
- Precomputation of all subarray metrics V[l][r]
- DP over prefixes with exactly k blocks
- Path reconstruction from predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space (reducible to O(nk))

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const V = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let mn = Infinity;
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      const sum = pref[r+1]-pref[l];
      V[l][r] = sum * mn;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const pre = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = NEG;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      pre[i][t] = pre[i-1][t];
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + V[j][i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; pre[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while(t>0){
    const j = pre[i][t];
    blocks.push([j, i-1]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you reduce memory to O(nk) with a streaming recomputation of V? 
- How would the approach adapt if k is large and n up to 2e5 (any pruning or heuristic)?

